  "long_speech": {
    "asset": "artifacts/voxcpm2/tiered-ane",
    "text": "The morning train arrived just as the first shops began to open. Mara stepped onto the platform with a small canvas bag and a notebook full of questions. She had come to the valley to record the stories of people who repaired old things. Her first appointment was with a clockmaker whose workshop stood beside the river. She followed the sound of running water through a narrow street, passing a bakery, a flower stall, and a bicycle leaning against a blue door.\n\nThe clockmaker welcomed her with a cup of tea. His workbench held tiny wheels, glass faces, wooden boxes, and several clocks waiting patiently for attention. He explained that every repair began with listening. A clock might run too quickly, stop at the same hour each day, or lose its voice altogether. Before he touched a tool, he watched its movement and learned its habits. Mara wrote this down, then closed her notebook so she could listen more carefully.\n\nAt noon they walked to the bakery. The baker was repairing a wooden chair that had belonged to her grandmother. One leg had split, but the seat was still strong. She showed Mara the marks left by years of use and explained why she wanted to keep them. A repaired object did not need to look new. It needed to be useful again, while keeping the parts of its history that mattered. Outside, a delivery bicycle rattled over the stones toward the market.\n\nIn the afternoon Mara visited the bicycle shop. The mechanic had arranged old frames along the wall and sorted spare parts into shallow drawers. A young customer arrived with a loose wheel and a worried expression. Together they checked the tire, tightened the axle, and tested the brakes. The mechanic asked the customer to explain each step before trying it. By the time the bicycle was ready, the worried expression had become a smile. Mara asked whether teaching always took longer. The mechanic said it usually saved time in the end.",
    "reference_seconds": 3.68,
    "seed": 23,
    "diffusion_steps": 10,
    "mode": "clone",
